WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.192
Module two. The vendor application, and KYC.

2
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:11.136
In this module, we'll go through the vendor application slowly, field by field.

3
00:00:11.586 --> 00:00:13.826
The six steps of the Apply to sell form.

4
00:00:14.276 --> 00:00:17.092
What every field means, and how the system checks it.

5
00:00:17.542 --> 00:00:19.633
The documents a vendor must upload.

6
00:00:20.083 --> 00:00:22.906
And the common mistakes that slow approval down.

7
00:00:22.906 --> 00:00:26.376
If you help vendors fill this form, this module is for you.

8
00:00:28.767 --> 00:00:32.413
A new vendor opens the vendor link and clicks Apply to sell.

9
00:00:32.413 --> 00:00:37.214
The form has six steps, and it takes about five minutes if the vendor has their

10
00:00:37.214 --> 00:00:38.004
Papers ready.

11
00:00:38.454 --> 00:00:39.926
One, business details.

12
00:00:40.376 --> 00:00:42.318
Two, the authorised signatory.

13
00:00:42.768 --> 00:00:44.410
Three, banking, for payouts.

14
00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:46.055
Four, the warehouse.

15
00:00:46.505 --> 00:00:48.702
Five, the categories they want to sell.

16
00:00:49.152 --> 00:00:52.416
And six, documents. Then they confirm KYC.

17
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:56.677
Step one is business details.

18
00:00:57.127 --> 00:01:02.533
The legal business name. Tell vendors to type it exactly as it appears on their GST

19
00:01:02.533 --> 00:01:03.314
Certificate.

20
00:01:03.764 --> 00:01:07.945
The entity type, such as private limited, partnership or proprietorship.

21
00:01:08.395 --> 00:01:10.657
The business PAN and the GSTIN.

22
00:01:11.107 --> 00:01:15.480
The CIN, which companies have, and the year the business was established.

23
00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:17.338
And the registered address.

24
00:01:19.733 --> 00:01:21.653
Let's watch this in the live demo.

25
00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:29.339
Here is the real form. On the left, you can see it's step one of six: Business.

26
00:01:35.867 --> 00:01:39.254
The legal business name goes first, then the entity type.

27
00:01:39.254 --> 00:01:41.691
In the demo, Private Limited is selected.

28
00:01:50.267 --> 00:01:54.896
Next come the PAN, the GSTIN and the CIN, and the year established.

29
00:02:03.067 --> 00:02:08.705
Look at the red message under GSTIN. The format is wrong, so the form shows the

30
00:02:08.705 --> 00:02:11.131
Expected fifteen character format.

31
00:02:20.667 --> 00:02:26.829
And here, a second check. A vendor with this GSTIN is already registered.

32
00:02:26.829 --> 00:02:30.459
One GSTIN can only be used once on Oribrix.

33
00:02:41.467 --> 00:02:46.489
So remember two checks on the GSTIN. It must be fifteen characters in the correct

34
00:02:46.489 --> 00:02:50.457
Format, and it must not already be registered by another vendor.

35
00:02:50.457 --> 00:02:55.478
If a vendor sees the already registered message, they may have applied before, so

36
00:02:55.478 --> 00:02:58.640
Check the admin panel before creating anything new.

37
00:03:38.633 --> 00:03:43.313
Step two is the authorised signatory. This is the person who can sign for the

38
00:03:43.313 --> 00:03:43.860
Business.

39
00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:46.998
Their full name, exactly as on their PAN card.

40
00:03:47.448 --> 00:03:50.541
Their designation, for example director or owner.

41
00:03:50.991 --> 00:03:52.954
Their Aadhaar and personal PAN.

42
00:03:53.404 --> 00:03:54.492
A mobile number.

43
00:03:54.942 --> 00:04:00.110
And an email. This email becomes the vendor's login, so it must be one that hasn't

44
00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:01.875
Been used on Oribrix before.

45
00:04:04.267 --> 00:04:06.187
Let's watch this in the live demo.

46
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:15.447
Now step two. The signatory's name is entered as per PAN, and the designation, here

47
00:04:15.447 --> 00:04:16.037
Director.

48
00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:30.928
Then the Aadhaar number, the personal PAN, and the mobile number.

49
00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:50.342
Last is the email. In the demo, the team had to try a few emails, because each one

50
00:04:50.342 --> 00:04:52.475
Had already been used for a login.

51
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:06.970
Step three is banking. This is where Oribrix sends the vendor's payouts, so it's

52
00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:11.877
Checked carefully. The account number must be nine to eighteen digits, and some

53
00:05:11.877 --> 00:05:16.847
Banks have a fixed length. In the demo, Bank of Baroda accepted exactly fourteen

54
00:05:16.847 --> 00:05:22.002
Digits. The IFSC code has eleven characters, and the fifth character must always be

55
00:05:22.002 --> 00:05:22.437
A zero.

56
00:06:09.167 --> 00:06:11.087
Let's watch this in the live demo.

57
00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:19.049
Here is the banking step. First the account holder's name.

58
00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:33.427
Then the bank is chosen from the list. Bank of Baroda is selected, and the account

59
00:06:33.427 --> 00:06:34.388
Number is typed.

60
00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:54.088
Now Canara Bank is tried. Watch the IFSC box. The message says the fifth character

61
00:06:54.088 --> 00:06:55.519
Of IFSC must be zero.

62
00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:11.753
Branch and account type finish this step. Tip: ask vendors to keep a cancelled

63
00:07:11.753 --> 00:07:14.367
Cheque handy, it has all of these details.

64
00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:27.313
Step four is the warehouse. For KYC, every vendor must show one real, physical

65
00:07:27.313 --> 00:07:30.655
Warehouse. This is where orders will be picked up.

66
00:07:31.105 --> 00:07:34.454
A warehouse name, and the storage capacity in square feet.

67
00:07:34.904 --> 00:07:38.948
The exact location on the map, using Locate me or the search box.

68
00:07:38.948 --> 00:07:41.624
Make sure the pin is on the right building.

69
00:07:42.074 --> 00:07:43.759
Opening and closing times.

70
00:07:44.209 --> 00:07:46.791
The vehicle types that can reach this warehouse.

71
00:07:47.241 --> 00:07:50.910
After KYC approval, the vendor can add more warehouses.

72
00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:57.467
Why do vehicle types matter? Because a big truck can't enter every lane.

73
00:07:57.467 --> 00:07:58.740
These are the options.

74
00:07:59.190 --> 00:08:02.475
Mini truck or pickup, for small loads and narrow lanes.

75
00:08:02.925 --> 00:08:04.376
Light commercial vehicles.

76
00:08:04.826 --> 00:08:06.426
Medium commercial vehicles.

77
00:08:06.876 --> 00:08:10.289
Heavy commercial vehicles, for bulk loads on wide roads.

78
00:08:10.739 --> 00:08:15.382
And trailers and containers. Only tick the vehicles that can really reach the

79
00:08:15.382 --> 00:08:16.286
Warehouse gate.

80
00:08:18.700 --> 00:08:20.620
Let's watch this in the live demo.

81
00:08:25.233 --> 00:08:29.372
Here's step four. The warehouse name and storage capacity come first.

82
00:08:39.633 --> 00:08:43.900
Locate me finds the current location, or you can search and drag the pin.

83
00:08:58.833 --> 00:09:04.667
Then the timings, and the allowed vehicle list: mini truck, LCV, MCV, HCV, and

84
00:09:04.667 --> 00:09:05.340
Trailers.

85
00:09:19.633 --> 00:09:24.631
In the demo, the warehouse is named Central City, with ten thousand square feet of

86
00:09:24.631 --> 00:09:25.180
Capacity.

87
00:09:38.033 --> 00:09:42.738
Step five is categories. This decides which products the vendor will be allowed to

88
00:09:42.738 --> 00:09:43.025
Sell.

89
00:09:43.475 --> 00:09:48.645
Standard categories are picked from the list: tiles, plumbing, electrical, tools,

90
00:09:48.645 --> 00:09:51.646
Roofing, safety equipment, concrete and bricks.

91
00:09:52.096 --> 00:09:57.573
If something is missing, the vendor clicks Other, types a name and uploads an image.

92
00:09:57.573 --> 00:10:00.768
A custom category goes to the admin for approval.

93
00:10:00.768 --> 00:10:04.810
Also note: some categories need brand authorisation documents.

94
00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:09.120
Let's watch this in the live demo.

95
00:10:13.733 --> 00:10:18.448
Here are the category cards. Concrete, bricks and safety equipment are selected.

96
00:10:29.733 --> 00:10:34.213
Now Other is clicked. A small window asks for a category name and an image.

97
00:10:50.533 --> 00:10:53.895
Outdoor sports now shows as a private custom category.

98
00:10:53.895 --> 00:10:55.888
It will wait for admin approval.

99
00:11:07.333 --> 00:11:11.621
Step six is documents. Four are mandatory and four are optional.

100
00:11:12.071 --> 00:11:17.711
The mandatory ones are the GST certificate, the business PAN, a cancelled cheque,

101
00:11:17.711 --> 00:11:22.098
And the owner's Aadhaar. Without these, KYC can't be confirmed.

102
00:11:22.548 --> 00:11:28.493
The optional ones are a trade licence, the Oribrix agreement, an ISO nine thousand

103
00:11:28.493 --> 00:11:31.974
One certificate, and MSME or Udyam registration.

104
00:11:31.974 --> 00:11:34.366
These can also be uploaded later.

105
00:11:36.767 --> 00:11:38.687
Let's watch this in the live demo.

106
00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:48.250
This is the documents step. Each row has an upload button, and every file is

107
00:11:48.250 --> 00:11:49.423
Encrypted at rest.

108
00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:08.391
Uploaded files show as selected, with a view and replace option.

109
00:12:08.391 --> 00:12:10.671
Optional rows are marked optional.

110
00:12:24.100 --> 00:12:29.646
After Confirm KYC, the documents show as received, and the status changes to In

111
00:12:29.646 --> 00:12:30.137
Review.

112
00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:45.956
After submitting, the vendor lands on this KYC and compliance control center.

113
00:12:46.406 --> 00:12:48.689
It shows how many documents are uploaded,

114
00:12:49.139 --> 00:12:53.213
The verification status, which stays In review until the admin acts,

115
00:12:53.663 --> 00:12:58.015
And the approval progress. Approvals usually land within twenty four hours.

116
00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:05.222
Here are the mistakes we see most often. Help vendors avoid them, and approval

117
00:13:05.222 --> 00:13:06.459
Becomes much faster.

118
00:13:06.909 --> 00:13:10.194
The business name doesn't match the GST certificate.

119
00:13:10.644 --> 00:13:13.801
The login email has already been used on Oribrix.

120
00:13:14.251 --> 00:13:17.217
A wrong IFSC code, or the wrong account number length.

121
00:13:17.667 --> 00:13:20.227
The warehouse pin is placed on the wrong building.

122
00:13:20.677 --> 00:13:25.583
And blurry or cut-off document photos. Ask for clear scans or PDFs.

123
00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:33.352
Quick check. A vendor typed the IFSC code H D F C one zero zero zero one two three.

124
00:13:33.352 --> 00:13:36.640
What's wrong with it? Pause and think for a moment.

125
00:13:40.590 --> 00:13:43.894
The answer: the fifth character of an IFSC must be zero.

126
00:13:43.894 --> 00:13:46.371
Here it's one, so the form will reject it.

127
00:13:49.167 --> 00:13:50.639
Let's sum up module two.

128
00:13:51.089 --> 00:13:56.399
The form has six steps: business, signatory, banking, warehouse, categories, and

129
00:13:56.399 --> 00:13:57.062
Documents.

130
00:13:57.512 --> 00:14:01.267
The GSTIN must be valid, and not already registered.

131
00:14:01.717 --> 00:14:05.536
The login email must be new, and the bank details are validated.

132
00:14:05.986 --> 00:14:08.460
Custom categories need admin approval.

133
00:14:08.910 --> 00:14:13.414
And all four mandatory documents are needed before confirming KYC.

134
00:14:13.414 --> 00:14:17.849
Next, we'll switch sides and see how the admin approves a vendor.

135
00:14:20.233 --> 00:14:25.588
That's the end of module 2. Next up, module 3: Admin: approving KYC.
